course: dictionary
lesson: 3337
title: Good, evil and the lesser of two evils
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Evil means very bad and cruel, wanting to hurt others. It can be an adjective, describing a person or thing, as in «an evil plan». It can also be a noun, meaning great wickedness and harm, or a very bad thing that causes harm.
explain: Read the words around evil to see which meaning fits. «Good and evil» is a pair of opposites: good is kind and right, and evil is cruel and wrong. In fairy tales, wicked witches and villains are evil characters who fight against good ones, and good usually wins.
explain: In everyday talk, evil is sometimes used playfully, as in «an evil grin». The grin is not really cruel; it is a sly, mischievous smile. Check your meaning by swapping in very bad and cruel. If it does not make sense, the writer means something else.
explain: The phrase «the lesser of two evils» means choosing the less harmful of two bad choices. Here evils is a noun, the plural of evil.
fact: Evil is an adjective and a noun; as a noun, its plural is evils.
fact: «Evil» means very bad and cruel, wanting to hurt others.
fact: «Good and evil» are opposites: good is kind and right, evil is cruel and wrong.
fact: «The lesser of two evils» means the less harmful of two bad choices.
fact: In everyday talk, «an evil grin» is playful, not really cruel.
exercise: «The villain made an evil plan to steal the king's crown.» What does evil mean here? | Very kind and helpful | *Very bad and cruel, wanting to hurt others | Very old and dusty | Very small and quiet
exercise: In «good and evil», which word is the opposite of kind and right? | Lesser | Grin | Plan | *Evil
exercise: In «the lesser of two evils», what does evils mean? | Stories about witches | Sly, playful grins | Two friends who are kind | *Bad things that cause harm
test: «He wore an evil grin when he saw the cake.» In this sentence, evil means: | Very old | *Sly and playful, not really cruel | Sad and tired | Very bad and cruel
test: Which phrase means choosing the less harmful of two bad choices? | An evil grin | Good and evil | *The lesser of two evils | An evil plan
exam: «Everyone who does evil hates the light.» Which meaning fits best here? | Very old | Sly and playful | *Very bad and cruel, wanting to hurt others | Short and rude
